---
/**
 * SCROLL CLUSTER — dither-star cluster resolving into constellation-like
 * symbols fading into 3D (spec §4.2). The shader does the visual work; the
 * DOM layer opens with two lines and the seven sisters, then the
 * Στοιχεῖον essay: letters, elements, heavenly bodies, and why the page
 * is tuned the way the old cosmos was tuned.
 */
import Section from '../Section.astro';
import type { SectionSpec } from '../../design/tokens';

interface Props {
  spec: SectionSpec;
}

const { spec } = Astro.props;
---

<Section spec={spec}>
  <p class="line">The stars were letters first.</p>
  <p class="line line--dim greek">Πλειάδες: seven, and the eye finds six.</p>
  <!-- The seven sisters: DOM nodes over the shader constellation. Each is an
       egg anchor; the natal-ascent sequence reads data-egg-step tokens.
       src/scripts/cluster.ts lands them in 7/8 as the section scrolls in,
       receiving the natal hero's scattered planets (--dx/--dy/--s/--o). -->
  <div class="pleiades" aria-hidden="true">
    {[
      { step: 0, x: 12, y: 18 }, { step: 1, x: 30, y: 6 },
      { step: 2, x: 46, y: 26 }, { step: 3, x: 58, y: 10 },
      { step: 4, x: 71, y: 32 }, { step: 5, x: 84, y: 14 },
      { step: 6, x: 93, y: 40 },
    ].map((n) => (
      <button
        class="node"
        type="button"
        tabindex="-1"
        data-egg-anchor="cluster-node"
        data-egg-step={String(n.step)}
        style={`left:${n.x}%; top:${n.y}%`}
      ></button>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p class="essay">
    The Greek says so in one word. <span class="greek" lang="grc">Στοιχεῖον</span>:
    a letter of the alphabet, a physical element, and, in the later grammar,
    a heavenly body. One wordform holding the alphabet, the material world,
    and the sky. Flatten the word and you lose the sentence it was keeping:
    matter is written, and the sky is the oldest text we still read.
  </p>
  <p class="essay">
    It was read for survival before it was read for meaning. Hesiod hands the
    farmer the sky as an instruction manual: when the Pleiades rise, daughters
    of Atlas, begin the harvest; when they set, the plough
    (<em>Works and Days</em>, 383). Seven sisters, and the eye finds six.
    Every alphabet keeps a letter the eye loses.
  </p>
  <p class="essay">
    Pythagoras heard the text. He wrote nothing; the doctrine reaches us
    through Aristotle, and Aristotle preserves the best part: the Pythagoreans
    held that the spinning bodies sound a harmony we fail to notice because it
    has played since the moment of our birth (<em>De Caelo</em> II.9). A
    sound present from every birth, for every generation, for the whole life
    of the species, stops registering as sound. It becomes structure. That is
    what I mean when I say the music of the spinning bodies speaks an
    evolutionary language to the soul: we were steeped in the signal before
    we had ears, and the signal did some of the writing.
  </p>
  <p class="essay">
    Plato scored it and spelled it. In the <em>Republic</em>, Er watches the
    cosmos turn with a Siren on each whorl, each singing one note, eight notes
    concording into a single harmony (X, 617b). In the <em>Timaeus</em> he
    goes further down the alphabet than anyone: fire, air, water, and earth
    are commonly called the <em>stoicheia</em>, the letters, and Plato objects
    that they are not even syllables. The true letters are the triangles
    beneath them, assembling into the five perfect solids, the characters
    matter is written in. The elements are words. The geometry is the
    alphabet.
  </p>
  <p class="essay">
    Kepler read Plato literally and nested those five solids between the six
    planetary orbits (<em>Mysterium Cosmographicum</em>, 1596). The model was
    wrong. He kept listening for twenty more years, and the harmony finally
    gave up a true law, the one that still holds every satellite over your
    head tonight (<em>Harmonices Mundi</em>, 1619). He heard the heavens as
    "a continuous song for several voices, perceived by the intellect," and
    he knew exactly whose mail he was opening: "God himself has waited six
    thousand years for a witness."
  </p>
  <p class="essay">
    That sentence carries the other thing I believe about ideas. Every idea
    already exists; the commons hold all of them, and an idea only borrows a
    person. It presents itself to someone, and if that someone sits on it, it
    withdraws and presents itself through another. The harmony of the spheres
    waited out Pythagoras's silence, Plato's myth, and two thousand years of
    better instruments until Kepler agreed to be the witness. Ideas are
    patient and promiscuous. The only exclusive claim you ever hold on one is
    execution, and the clock on that claim is always running. I build the way
    I do because I've watched ideas I slept on wake up in other hands.
  </p>
  <p class="essay">
    So this page is tuned, the way the old cosmos was tuned. The sections
    modulate by fifths. The timing counts in sevens. And somewhere in the
    house, a knock keeps seven-eight time. The music is only inaudible while
    it never stops.
  </p>
</Section>

<style>
  .pleiades {
    position: relative;
    height: 9rem;
    max-width: 28rem;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 2.8rem;
  }

  .essay {
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }

  .node {
    position: absolute;
    width: 14px;
    height: 14px;
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: radial-gradient(circle, var(--section-accent) 0%, transparent 70%);
    /* Arrival state, written by cluster.ts: offset from the hero above,
       scale and opacity ramping as each sister takes her station. */
    --dx: 0px;
    --dy: 0px;
    --s: 1;
    --o: 0.55;
    --hover: 1;
    opacity: var(--o);
    transform: translate(var(--dx), var(--dy)) scale(calc(var(--s) * var(--hover)));
    cursor: default;
    transition: opacity 0.4s ease, transform 0.4s ease;
    will-change: transform, opacity;
  }

  .node:hover {
    --hover: 1.5;
    opacity: 1;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .node {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  .line {
    font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 2.6vw, 1.6rem);
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
  }

  .line--dim {
    font-size: clamp(0.9rem, 2vw, 1.15rem);
    color: var(--ink-dim);
  }
</style>
